    "narrative": "For this August 21st matchup, our offensive core is headlined by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who boasts the day\u2019s premier score of 76.07. He benefits from an elite combination of a favorable hitter\u2019s park and a slow opposing delivery that significantly boosts his stolen base probability. Similarly, **Dylan Beavers** (68.38) and **Lawrence Butler** (68.15) provide massive upside; **Dylan Beavers** leverages a significant park factor and stolen base opportunity, while **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a strong platoon advantage against Wesneski. **Bryce Harper** remains a steady high-floor anchor at 67.36, bolstered by his prime spot in the order and a favorable xwOBA trend, making him a must-start despite facing a decent arm in Dobbins.\n\nStrategic platoon edges and park dynamics are heavily influencing the configuration. **JJ Bleday** (66.17) is a strong play today, fueled by a massive +16% skill boost against a vulnerable Rodriguez, and **Daulton Varsho** (65.61) is firing on all cylinders due to a +10% platoon edge. **Gleyber Torres** (64.16) has also been elevated into a prominent role; while his base projection is moderate, the combination of leading off and a significant platoon edge against a lefty makes him one of our most efficient options. Conversely, **Brendan Donovan** is the most volatile asset in the starting nine; his score of 47.78 is carried almost entirely by his leadoff status, as a -15% platoon penalty against Boyd drags his value toward the start-sit threshold.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Zack Gelof**, who hit a respectable 55.18 but ultimately landed on the bench. While his score is respectable, he narrowly missed the cut because the current outfield and utility slots provided higher efficiency through superior platoon matchups and park-adjusted run-scoring environments. We are continuing to monitor the health of **Will Smith**, **Tyler Freeman**, and **Jonathan Aranda**, who remain unavailable, 